1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,743
Every simulation so far has run on seven
numbers: resistance, two inductances,

2
00:00:05,783 --> 00:00:10,262
the magnet's flux linkage, inertia, and
two kinds of friction.

3
00:00:10,302 --> 00:00:15,081
But a real motor often arrives with three
wires, a shaft, and no datasheet.

4
00:00:15,121 --> 00:00:17,850
Today we measure all seven, with a bench
supply,

5
00:00:17,890 --> 00:00:20,525
a scope, a current probe and a drill.

6
00:00:21,625 --> 00:00:23,822
First, Lesson 14's answers.

7
00:00:23,862 --> 00:00:36,461
One: v_d = −ωe L i_q: −0.5 V; v_q = R i_q
+ ωe λm: 6.9 V.

8
00:00:36,501 --> 00:00:44,015
Two: L over R, 0.05 mH over 40 mΩ:
1.25 ms.

9
00:00:44,055 --> 00:00:48,728
Three: R is V over I once the current
settles; L is R times τ,

10
00:00:48,768 --> 00:00:53,144
the time to reach 63 %. That's today's
second test.

11
00:00:54,244 --> 00:00:58,773
The parameters split in two: electrical,
R, Ld, Lq and λm;

12
00:00:58,813 --> 00:01:04,765
and mechanical: inertia J, viscous
friction B, and Coulomb friction Tc.

13
00:01:04,805 --> 00:01:09,047
Five tests find them: four-wire
resistance, a voltage step with the

14
00:01:09,087 --> 00:01:13,729
rotor locked, the open-circuit EMF, a run-
down, and a torque check.

15
00:01:13,769 --> 00:01:17,186
Every trace you'll see is synthetic:
generated from our motor's

16
00:01:17,226 --> 00:01:21,184
model with noise, so each fit can be
checked against the truth.

17
00:01:22,284 --> 00:01:26,485
Test one: drive 2 A into terminals a and
b, and read the voltage

18
00:01:26,525 --> 00:01:31,259
with separate leads at the terminals:
Lesson 2's four-wire method.

19
00:01:31,299 --> 00:01:37,544
In star, that's two phases in series:
162.3 mV at 24 °C.

20
00:01:37,584 --> 00:01:42,883
With the other pairs, R_LL averages
81.2 mΩ.

21
00:01:42,923 --> 00:01:47,827
Half of that is the star-equivalent phase
resistance, even for a delta winding.

22
00:01:47,867 --> 00:01:57,379
Correct to 20 °C: divide by 1 + 0.393 %/K
× 4 K. R = 40.0 mΩ.

23
00:01:58,479 --> 00:02:04,069
Test two: inductance. First, park the
rotor: DC into a, out of b and c together.

24
00:02:04,109 --> 00:02:09,747
Its field points along phase a, and the
magnet's north pole swings onto it.

25
00:02:09,787 --> 00:02:12,991
Now the d axis sits on phase a. Clamp the
shaft there.

26
00:02:13,031 --> 00:02:18,530
A voltage step from a to b and c drives
current only along d:

27
00:02:18,570 --> 00:02:21,927
the supply sees 1.5 R and 1.5 Ld.

28
00:02:21,967 --> 00:02:26,533
A step from b to c, with a open, drives
current only along q:

29
00:02:26,573 --> 00:02:31,982
2R and 2Lq. But q current makes torque, so
the clamp has to hold.

30
00:02:33,082 --> 00:02:38,606
Apply 0.3 V from a to b and c, and record
the voltage and the current.

31
00:02:38,646 --> 00:02:45,791
The fit gives 5.0 A and τ = 1.25 ms: a
60 mΩ loop,

32
00:02:45,831 --> 00:02:53,951
and τ times that, 75 µH. Divide by 1.5: Ld
= 0.050 mH.

33
00:02:53,991 --> 00:03:00,188
The q step, 0.4 V from b to c, gives the
same 1.25 ms:

34
00:03:00,228 --> 00:03:05,323
Lq = 0.050 mH, as surface magnets should.

35
00:03:05,363 --> 00:03:10,158
Without the clamp, the rotor turns, its
back-EMF fights the supply,

36
00:03:10,198 --> 00:03:14,594
and the current peaks at 2.7 A: no
exponential to fit.

37
00:03:15,694 --> 00:03:17,622
Test three: the magnet. Unclamp,

38
00:03:17,662 --> 00:03:21,343
open all three leads, and spin the shaft
with a drill.

39
00:03:21,383 --> 00:03:27,686
Scope the line-to-line voltage. Nine
periods take 92.4 ms:

40
00:03:27,726 --> 00:03:32,460
97.4 Hz, so the shaft turns at 1461 rpm.

41
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:37,871
Fit the fundamental, not the raw peak:
6.89 V.

42
00:03:37,911 --> 00:03:43,799
Divide by √3 ωe: λm = 6.50 mWb, as in
Lesson 7.

43
00:03:43,839 --> 00:03:49,968
So the torque constant, 3/2 p λm, should
be 39.0 mN·m per amp.

44
00:03:51,068 --> 00:03:55,753
Test four: the mechanics. Spin the motor
to 3000 rpm with any drive,

45
00:03:55,793 --> 00:03:58,654
then disconnect it and let it coast.

46
00:03:58,694 --> 00:04:02,765
With the leads open, no current flows,

47
00:04:02,805 --> 00:04:08,358
so only friction slows it: J dω/dt = −(Bω
+ Tc).

48
00:04:08,398 --> 00:04:11,188
Record the EMF all the way down:

49
00:04:11,228 --> 00:04:16,895
each electrical period gives one speed
sample, 238 in 2.6 s.

50
00:04:16,935 --> 00:04:21,056
Viscous friction alone would decay
exponentially, Coulomb alone in a

51
00:04:21,096 --> 00:04:25,818
straight line; the real curve lies
between, and stops in finite time.

52
00:04:26,918 --> 00:04:34,078
To separate them, divide by J and plot
deceleration against speed:

53
00:04:34,118 --> 00:04:38,951
−dω/dt = (B/J) ω + Tc/J, a straight line.

54
00:04:38,991 --> 00:04:44,659
Fitting the closed-form solution gives the
slope, B/J:

55
00:04:44,699 --> 00:04:52,353
0.333 per second, and the intercept at
zero speed, Tc/J: 66.6 rad/s².

56
00:04:52,393 --> 00:04:54,341
But every term is divided by J:

57
00:04:54,381 --> 00:04:59,114
twice the inertia with twice the friction
coasts down identically.

58
00:05:00,214 --> 00:05:03,979
So we need one more fact: a known torque,
or a known inertia.

59
00:05:04,019 --> 00:05:09,501
Bolt on a steel disc, 50 mm across and
10 mm thick:

60
00:05:09,541 --> 00:05:16,613
½ m r² = 4.8 × 10⁻⁵ kg·m². Run down again:
it now takes 7.4 s.

61
00:05:16,653 --> 00:05:22,745
Friction hasn't changed, so both numbers
fall by the inertia ratio.

62
00:05:22,785 --> 00:05:28,179
J = 3.00 and B = 1.00, both × 10⁻⁵, and Tc
= 2.00 mN·m.

63
00:05:28,219 --> 00:05:36,321
Or use a drive that holds a known current:
Kt i_q = J dω/dt + Bω + Tc.

64
00:05:37,421 --> 00:05:39,713
Test five: park the rotor again,

65
00:05:39,753 --> 00:05:45,214
hold it with a 10 cm arm on a kitchen
scale, and drive DC from b to c.

66
00:05:45,254 --> 00:05:51,175
That current is all on q: the torque is √3
p λm, 45 mN·m per amp:

67
00:05:51,215 --> 00:05:56,527
the line-to-line EMF constant, by energy
conservation.

68
00:05:56,567 --> 00:06:01,996
Measure with plus and minus currents and
take half the difference:

69
00:06:02,036 --> 00:06:08,943
cogging and scale offset cancel. λm =
6.48 mWb, within 0.3 % of the drill test.

70
00:06:08,983 --> 00:06:16,432
So Kt = 3/2 p λm holds: 38.9 mN·m per amp,
against 39.0 from the EMF.

71
00:06:17,532 --> 00:06:20,782
Here's the whole sequence, and what the
records gave back.

72
00:06:20,822 --> 00:06:24,958
Every parameter within 0.3 %: that's the
fits on clean,

73
00:06:24,998 --> 00:06:29,459
synthetic data. On a real motor, expect a
few percent.

74
00:06:29,499 --> 00:06:33,610
The release folder has the five records
and a script that runs every fit,

75
00:06:33,650 --> 00:06:35,969
ready for your own motor's data.

76
00:06:37,069 --> 00:06:40,469
Where does this stop? Inductance depends
on current:

77
00:06:40,509 --> 00:06:43,976
iron saturates, and on d the magnet
already loads it.

78
00:06:44,016 --> 00:06:48,941
So step at a few currents, or inject a
small sine, like an LCR meter.

79
00:06:48,981 --> 00:06:51,507
Eddy currents in magnets and rotor iron
bend

80
00:06:51,547 --> 00:06:54,647
the start of the step away from an
exponential.

81
00:06:54,687 --> 00:06:57,341
The run-down's friction includes iron
loss:

82
00:06:57,381 --> 00:07:01,875
hysteresis acts like Coulomb friction,
eddy currents like viscous.

83
00:07:01,915 --> 00:07:09,002
Temperature changes R by 0.393 % and λm by
about −0.12 % per kelvin.

84
00:07:09,042 --> 00:07:17,612
Below 150 rpm, cogging and static friction
take over, so the fit stops there.

85
00:07:18,712 --> 00:07:20,992
Three predictions before next time.

86
00:07:21,032 --> 00:07:26,191
One: with only viscous friction, a rotor
coasts from 3000

87
00:07:26,231 --> 00:07:31,930
to 1500 rpm in 2.0 s. With J = 3 × 10⁻⁵
kg·m², what is B?

88
00:07:32,830 --> 00:07:39,776
Two: the line-to-line EMF peaks at 14.1 V
at 3000 rpm. What is λm?

89
00:07:40,676 --> 00:07:44,852
Three: why do three Hall sensors suffice
for a BLDC drive?

90
00:07:45,752 --> 00:07:51,708
Next time: the BLDC six-step drive, where
those Hall sensors decide when to switch.
